Dwarves are the earthiest species and some view that as
being less compatible with dieties.  Alternately, in
the Norse law that the Valkyrie pantheon is drawn from
they already are spirit beings on the order of elves
so they'd be priests to themselves.  In the case of
both dwarves and elves, Nethack does not appear to have
drawn their features from the Norse lore.

> YANI: Dwarven priests ;)

Note that dwarvish priests exist in Eragon/Eldest the
new fantasy series.  I figure earth elementals would
always be generated friendly but other than that a
similar pattern.  They should be able to progress to
strength 25 without power gloves.

> Dwarves are limited to 16 int/wis and have the worst racial modifiers
> for power, reducing one of the class's notable benefits. It seems to me
> no sillier than an orcish wizard though.

Let's think about what monsters have spell attacks:

kobold shaman, orc shaman, gnomish wizard, golden naga,
barrow wight, titan, and assorted A+L+@+& and a couple of
D who are in quests.

Naga is insufficiently humanoid for armor items, titan
too large for body armor, and barrow wights don't leave
corpses so they wouldn't leave bones files.  A, L and &
share the no-corpse-therefore-no-bones issue (which I
suspect I just made up out of thin air).  So none of
those are good candidates for player species.  But a
few people like to ascend characters who are some monster
type the entire game using a poly-patch from last year
and they'd be reasonable for that purpose.

Looks to me like the species list that could become
wizards should be kobold, orc, gnome, human and elf.

As weak as kobolds start, it would be a major challenge
to get one to survive to complete the mines and Sokoban.
YANI - kobolds are an option for a player species.  Lower
max stats than any other species in at least a couple of
stat slots.  Like orcish wizard, just another way to
have a harder game for the folks who've already ascended
a bunch of characters.



